<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hanzhong</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hanzhong is a city in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaanxi">Shaanxi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, in the basin of the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-river">Han River</a> between the Qinling and Daba mountains. In 206 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiang-yu">Xiang Yu</a> made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bang">Liu Bang</a> King of Han there, and from it Liu Bang broke out to found the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a>, which took its name from the region. Its strategic passes were later contested between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bei">Liu Bei</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-cao">Cao Cao</a>, who lost it to Liu Bei in 219.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hanzhong.jpg" alt="Hanzhong" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Charlie fong · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Skyline_of_West_Hanzhong.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Hanzhong is a prefecture-level city in the south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaanxi">Shaanxi</a> province, bordering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a> to the south and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu">Gansu</a> to the west. Its name means middle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-river">Han River</a>. It lies in the centre of the Hanzhong Basin on the banks of the Han River, the largest tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a>, with the Daba Mountains to the south and the Qin Mountains to the north, which form the northern limit of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. The Qin Mountains shield the city from cold winter winds, so its winters are warmer than those at similar latitudes to the east.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>References to Hanzhong before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> unification of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> are few. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-documents">Book of Documents</a> speaks of an area called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wuwei">Liangzhou</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sima-qian">Sima Qian</a> of a Bao state, both believed to refer to it. The area has been called Nanzheng since about 900 BCE; one account says that people of the Western Zhou state of Zheng fled south after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-huan-of-zheng">Duke Huan of Zheng</a> was slain by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quanrong">Quanrong</a>, though the veracity of the story is controversial. Under the Qin it was the Hanzhong Commandery.</p><p>When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin dynasty</a> collapsed, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bang">Liu Bang</a>, later the founding emperor of the Han, was made lord of Hanzhong and spent several years there before raising an army against his rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiang-yu">Xiang Yu</a> in the Chu-Han contention. He named his dynasty after the region rather than his birthplace, Pei County, so that Hanzhong gave its name to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a>. In the later Eastern Han, Zhang Lu led an independent theocratic government there backed by the Way of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/way-of-the-five-pecks-of-rice">Celestial Masters</a>, until he surrendered Hanzhong to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-cao">Cao Cao</a> after the Battle of Yangping.</p><p>The region lay on the route from the Central Plain to the Sichuan Basin, which made it strategically important in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-kingdoms-period">Three Kingdoms period</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-cao">Cao Cao</a> lost it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bei">Liu Bei</a>, who took the title of King of Hanzhong, and the tomb of the Shu Han chancellor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhuge-liang">Zhuge Liang</a> remains there. In 784 Emperor Dezong of Tang fled to Hanzhong when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> was captured, and under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Northern Song</a> its tax revenue was just behind that of regional capitals such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chengdu">Chengdu</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming dynasty</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wanli-emperor">Wanli Emperor</a> installed his fifth son, Zhu Changhao, as king of Hanzhong, and he built a large palace; it was looted when he fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a> in 1643 before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-zicheng">Li Zicheng</a>'s rebel army. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-liberation-army">People's Liberation Army</a> took Hanzhong in December 1949. The city is centred on the Hantai District and has a museum, the ancient Hutou Bridge and the Zhangqian Memorial Museum among its sights.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hanzhong” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Against the Liang the Wei took Xinyang and <b>Hanzhong</b> by 505, but Wei Rui took Hefei in 506 and with Cao Jingzong destroyed the Wei at the Battle of Zhongli, ending large-scale war for years.</blockquote><a href="/empire/northern-wei">Northern Wei · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Yet it suffered major defeats: Qin took <b>Hanzhong</b> in 312 BCE, and the Qin general Bai Qi captured Ying in 278 BCE and burned the royal tombs at Yiling.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-chu">State of Chu · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>His grandson Zhang Lu ruled a theocratic state in the <b>Hanzhong</b> valley until he submitted to the warlord Cao Cao in 215.</blockquote><a href="/religion/daoism">Daoism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>When Liu Bang broke out of <b>Hanzhong</b> in 206 BCE, Dong Yi surrendered and his kingdom became Han territory.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-di-206-bc">Kingdom of Di (206 BC)</a></li><li><blockquote>Xiang Yu, wary of him, made him King of Han in 206 BCE, ruling the isolated <b>Hanzhong</b>, Ba and Shu regions from Nanzheng.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-han-206-bc">Kingdom of Han (206 BC)</a></li><li><blockquote>When Liu Bang broke out of <b>Hanzhong</b> in 206 BCE, Sima Xin surrendered and his land became Han territory.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-sai">Kingdom of Sai</a></li></ul></section>
